Solitude — City Profile
Atmosphere & Core Conflict (source, unchanged — this describes Skyrim's
general political temperature before any civil war, which is exactly
where this prequel sits): Solitude is a city at its peak — majestic,
orderly, prosperous. The air is crisp with sea salt and clean stone. The
core conflict is Imperial Order vs. Nordic Heart: the city's public face
is law and discipline, but beneath it, Nords grumble about the Thalmor
and the ban on Talos worship. Held in check by deep respect for the
young High King.

THE MARKET DISTRICT
Atmosphere: The vibrant heart of Solitude — fresh bread, spices, clean
leather, a Bards College student strumming somewhere nearby, calm
professional guard patrols.
Key NPCs: Sayma (proprietor, Bits and Pieces, general goods — prefers
gossip and road-news over extra coin). Angeline Morrard (elderly Breton
alchemist, Angeline's Aromatics — worried about her daughter in the
Legion, grateful for reassurance). Fihada (Bosmer master bowyer,
Fletcher — respects fellow artisans, better prices for demonstrated
skill). Lisette (ambitious bard, often near the Winking Skeever, wants a
new story). Evette San (nervous spiced-wine merchant, convinced a rival
sabotaged her shipment).
Opportunities: Shopping and upgrades at the three shops above. Gathering
rumors from Lisette or Evette San.

THE TEMPLE DISTRICT
Atmosphere: The Temple of the Divines — quiet reverence, incense, a
single struggling Gildergreen sapling in the garden.
Key NPCs: Freir (head priestess), assorted priests and acolytes.
Opportunities: Blessings at any of the eight shrines. Research on
religious history, the undead, or Daedric lore — dogma-filtered; library
access requires real persuasion.

CASTLE DOUR (THE MILITARY DISTRICT)
Atmosphere: A grim, functional Imperial Legion fortress. Sharper,
colder air; marching feet, steel on steel from the training yard.
Key NPCs: General Tullius and Legate Rikke (observed at drills or in
strategy). Disciplined Imperial soldiers and officers throughout.
Opportunities: Reporting a crime (bureaucratic, skeptical without
proof). Observing drills (insight into Legion tactics and discipline).

THE SOLITUDE DOCKS
Atmosphere: Salt, fish, gulls, sailors hauling cargo, trade-company
banners on the piers.
Key NPCs: Sailors and dockworkers (rough, transient, good for rumors
from other ports). Addvar (local fisherman, mending nets).
Opportunities: Rumors of coastal raids, strange weather, news from other
provinces.

THE HALL OF THE DEAD
Atmosphere: Solitude's catacombs, set into the rock face. Cool, still,
somber.
Key NPCs: Priests of Arkay.
Opportunities: Burial rites, local disease patterns, any unusual recent
deaths.

THE BLUE PALACE
Atmosphere: Imperial grandeur — polished stone, soaring arches,
stained-glass light, courtiers in fine silks, Legionnaires at attention.
Key NPCs: Jarl Elisif the Fair (the High King's wife, often holding
court). Falk Firebeard (her steward, busy, serious). Sybille Stentor
(court wizard, reclusive, usually in her chambers). Assorted thanes,
courtiers, servants. ADDED this session: Valerius Caelus (full
character: Toryggs legacy/Npcs/Valerius) — a Caelus-line noble working
Elisif's confidence and the Pale Court. Nothing in his own file dates
when this began; present here as an already-established fixture of the
court, well before any Toryggs Legacy timeline. He does not need to be
IN the palace to be encountered — see adventure2, "A Man Who Notices
Things," for where the party actually meets him.
Opportunities [REFRAMED — source ties this to preparing for a royal
audience that doesn't happen in this campaign; below is the palace as
ordinary sightseeing]: The palace is open to visitors during the day.
Glean courtly gossip from courtiers or servants (Guile roll) — political
tension, Elisif's private worries, Tullius's frustration with the
Thalmor. Consult Sybille Stentor on magical matters if she can be
bothered — she's curt with people who waste her time.

THE BARDS COLLEGE
Atmosphere: Flute from an upper window, a boisterous tavern song in the
courtyard, a practice drum. Old books, lute wood, beeswax.
Key NPCs: Viarmo (Headmaster, Altmer, dramatic, always hunting new
stories for the annual festival). Pantea Ateia, Giraud Gemane, Inge Six
Fingers (instructors and loremasters). Numerous bard students.
Opportunities: The best historical/mythological research in Solitude —
ancient symbols, forgotten legends, Dragon Cult history (a character
tracking Atmoran notation could plausibly come here for cross-reference,
though Wulfgar in Windhelm remains the established primary resource).
Commissioning a song to spread information. Impressing an instructor for
a reward or a piece of lore.

THE WINKING SKEEVER (INN)
Atmosphere: The social heart of the market district — warm, cozy,
off-duty Legionnaires, sailors' tall tales, Lisette's music.
Key NPCs: Corpulus Vinius (cheerful, well-connected innkeeper). Lisette
(resident bard). Off-duty Legionnaires. Traveling merchants.
Opportunities: The single best general rumor mill in Solitude. Hiring
short-term help or a distraction. Resting, rooms, gear storage — the
party's likely base of operations, and the location for Ismara's inn
scene (adventure2, "Ismara's Note").

RADIANT RAIMENT (TAILOR & FINE GOODS)
Atmosphere: High fashion, Imperial taste — expensive perfume, dyed
silks, mannequins, appraising eyes.
Key NPCs: Taarie (public-facing, haughty, appearance-obsessed). Endarie
(the true artist, meticulous, less patient than her sister).
[NOTE — Ismara's Doom cross-reference: this is Caelan the Fox's
daughters' shop, per `Toryggs legacy/Npcs/Caelan the fox`, Section VII.
He does not reveal this connection this adventure — see adventure2,
"Arrival — the Shopfront."]
Opportunities: The only place in the city for Fine Clothes. Taarie is a
conduit for court gossip (a difficult Guile roll, flattery-gated).
Haggling only responds to flattery about their craft and taste.

THE LONELY TROLL (DOCKSIDE TAVERN)
Atmosphere: The docks' drunken, salty gut — cheap ale, unwashed
sailors, spilled fish stew, sticky floor, frequent fights.
Key NPCs: Gulum-Ei (shifty Argonian informant, low-level Thieves Guild
contact). A motley crew of sailors.
Opportunities: Underworld rumors — smuggling routes, Thieves Guild
activity, ships with "problems" in their cargo. Starting a distraction-
brawl cheaply.

THE EAST EMPIRE COMPANY WAREHOUSE
Atmosphere: A massive wooden fortress of commerce — foreign spices,
tanned leather, armed guards, scribes checking manifests.
Key NPCs: Orthus Endario (stressed Imperial officer, no time for
anyone without an appointment). East Empire Wardens (private security,
better-equipped than City Guard).
Opportunities: Shipment records (very high Guile roll or a real bribe).
Infiltration — a major stealth challenge, well-guarded day and night.

THE FOUR SHIELDS TAVERN
Atmosphere: Old Skyrim transplanted into the Imperial capital —
woodsmoke, roasting meat, strong mead, hunting trophies, almost
exclusively Nord patrons.
Key NPCs: Odfel (one-eyed proprietor, lost his eye in the Great War,
traditionalist, resentful of Imperial weakness). Rotating traditionalist
Nord regulars.
Opportunities: The best place in the city for anti-Imperial sentiment,
Thalmor grievances, pro-Stormcloak sympathy (background texture only —
no civil war exists yet in this timeline; play as grumbling, not
recruitment). Cold reception for openly pro-Imperial characters; warmer
for anyone who respects Nord tradition. Haggling favors demonstrated
Nord loyalty/respect.

THE GILDED SCROLL (MAGICAL GOODS & ENCHANTING)
Atmosphere: A quiet side street near the Blue Palace — dim, old
parchment, exotic reagents, a humming Arcane Enchanter.
Key NPCs: Horatius (anxious Imperial apprentice, terrified of his
master). Sybille Stentor (rarely present in person; felt through
Horatius's nervous mentions of "the Mistress").
Opportunities: Soul gems, enchanting materials, a limited selection of
low-tier spell tomes. Recharging and basic enchanting for a fee; custom
work requires "consultation with the Mistress." Haggling favors
demonstrated arcane knowledge.

CASTLE DOUR DUNGEON (THE CONSEQUENCE)
Atmosphere: Cold, damp cells beneath Castle Dour — mildew, a single
sputtering torch, distant groans.
Key NPCs: A grim, silent jailor.
Opportunities (how to get out): Paying a bounty. Lockpicking (Hard -2
Guile). Serving a minor sentence (costs the rest of the day's actions).
A full jailbreak (stealth and combat against dungeon guards; marks the
party as wanted in Haafingar).

City Events Table (d20)
GM Note: roll once during downtime if the party is unsure what to do or
you want a complication.
1-5   Quiet Day — calm, orderly, maximum freedom for personal business.
6-7   Legion Drill — a full cohort drilling in the main square. Crosses
      the city are harder; stealth is harder near the square.
8-9   Thalmor Presence — three Justiciars moving toward the Blue Palace,
      arrogant, watched with open hatred. City tension spikes.
10-11 Dockside Brawl — a ship's crew and local Nords fighting at the
      port; guards struggling to contain it. Good cover for slipping
      away unnoticed.
12-13 Market Day — a Khajiit caravan sets up temporary stalls. Rare
      alchemical ingredients and one minor enchanted item, today only.
14    [ADAPTED from source's Stormcloak-recruit event, which presumes a
      civil war not yet underway here] A Traveler's Warning — a farmer
      newly arrived from the Pale is telling anyone who'll listen about
      people standing frozen in fields on the road north. Most of the
      crowd treats it as a drunk's story. A few don't laugh.
16    Bards College Recital — an open-air recital in the palace garden;
      nobles in attendance; Advantage on Persuasion rolls made there.
17    A Desperate Plea — an NPC (a child, an elderly person) asks the
      party directly for help with something small and immediate.
18    Public Execution — a criminal executed in Castle Dour's courtyard;
      a large, somber crowd; a reminder of Imperial justice.
19    Famous Visitor — a renowned figure visiting the Jarl adds
      complexity or opportunity to a palace interaction.
20    Sudden Storm — a fierce storm off the sea forces everyone indoors
      for a few hours; inns packed; good setup for an all-in-one-room
      social scene.
[REMOVED from source: "Royal Decree" (referenced specific Toryggs
Legacy political events — "disturbances in other holds" — with no
equivalent here to reference); no replacement needed, roll again or
treat as Quiet Day if it comes up.]
